# Environment variables for the Adaptive Learning Path app.
# Populated per-feature as we add integrations. Copy to .env.local (git-ignored) and fill in.

# --- Phase 1: Vertex AI (Gemini) ---
# GCP setup steps: see Feature 1a in docs/ROADMAP.md.
# CREDENTIALS_JSON is the FULL service-account JSON as a SINGLE LINE
# (no real newlines — `\n` inside the private_key string is fine and required).
# Flatten a downloaded key with:  jq -c . path/to/sa.json
# Paste the resulting one-line string verbatim into .env.local and into the
# Vercel dashboard env-var field. Real newlines will break JSON.parse at boot.
# OPTIONAL since Workers Block B: when unset, Vertex auth falls back to
# Application Default Credentials — the containerized course worker runs with
# it unset and authenticates as the Cloud Run runtime service account (no key
# JSON in the image; see Dockerfile.worker for the worker's full env contract).
# Local dev and Vercel keep setting it.
GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT=
GOOGLE_VERTEX_LOCATION=us-central1
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON=
# Region for Anthropic Claude partner models (Vertex Model Garden). Claude is
# NOT served from us-central1, so it gets its own location. Defaults to the
# `global` endpoint; override to a Claude region (e.g. us-east5) if needed.
GOOGLE_VERTEX_ANTHROPIC_LOCATION=global
# Region for Gemini 3.x models. These are served from the `global` endpoint, not
# regional ones like us-central1 (a regional call 404s). Defaults to `global`.
GOOGLE_VERTEX_GEMINI3_LOCATION=global

# --- Phase 1: Postgres (Prisma) ---
# DATABASE_URL is the app's RUNTIME connection. In local development it points at
# the LOCAL Docker Postgres, not Supabase — an earlier version of this file said
# the opposite, and it was wrong. Local is where the integration tests write and
# self-clean and where the compose workers poll; aiming either at production
# would have them writing to, and draining the queue of, the live DB.
# Local value (matches docker-compose.yml):
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:55432/learning_app
# In production DATABASE_URL is the Supabase TRANSACTION-POOLER URL (port 6543,
# ?sslmode=require) — Supabase → Project Settings → Database → Connection string.
#
# To run a script against production, override inline for that one command —
# shell env beats `--env-file`, so this is reliable and leaves .env.local alone:
#   DATABASE_URL="$SUPABASE_POOLER_URL" npx tsx --env-file=.env.local scripts/<x>.ts
# Never point .env.local's DATABASE_URL at Supabase to achieve the same thing:
# the setting outlives the command, and the next `npm run test:int` or compose
# worker inherits it.
#
# SUPABASE_POOLER_URL is that override's source — the same 6543 pooler URL
# production runs on. Use it, NOT SUPABASE_DB_URL below: that one is the direct
# 5432 endpoint, which is IPv6-only on current Supabase projects, so it works
# from a laptop and fails from anything IPv4-only (Cloud Build hit exactly this,
# with an opaque P1001). It is also the migration connection, not the runtime
# one. Uncomment and fill this in locally if you operate against production —
# left empty it expands to nothing, and since a set-but-empty shell variable
# still beats `--env-file`, the script aborts rather than quietly running
# against local.
# SUPABASE_POOLER_URL='postgresql://…:6543/postgres?sslmode=require'
#
# DIRECT_URL is consumed by the Prisma CLI for migrations and `db pull` (see
# prisma.config.ts). For local development this MUST point at the local Docker
# Postgres so `prisma migrate dev` never touches prod. See docs/db-setup.md.
# Local value (matches docker-compose.yml):
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:55432/learning_app
# In Vercel, DIRECT_URL is set to the Supabase direct connection (port 5432) so
# `prisma migrate deploy` can run during build.
#
# --- Free-beta D2: one-shot library migration (scripts/migrate-library.ts) ---
# SUPABASE_DB_URL is the COPY TARGET — the Supabase DIRECT connection (5432, not
# the 6543 pooler, which rewrites session state under a long-running client). It
# is a separate variable from DATABASE_URL on purpose: the script copies local →
# Supabase, and keeping source and target in different variables means the copy
# direction can't be reversed by editing this file. Deliberately NOT in
# .env.local — pass it inline for the one run:
#   SUPABASE_DB_URL='postgresql://…:5432/postgres?sslmode=require' \
#     npx tsx --env-file=.env.local scripts/migrate-library.ts --yes
# Scoped to that one script. For every other ops override use
# SUPABASE_POOLER_URL above.
# SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# --- Phase 2d/3b: dev auth bypass ---
# Set to "1" to let withAuth / withAdminAuth / the playground pages run WITHOUT
# a real session (userId null). Local-only: ignored unless NODE_ENV=development,
# so it is inert in any deployed build. A real Supabase session (Phase 3b vars
# below) takes precedence when present.
#
# Free-beta E2 retired this from the OPERATOR path: the curation skills now
# authenticate with OPERATOR_ADMIN_TOKEN below, against whichever service
# OPERATOR_BASE_URL names. DEV_AUTH remains only for local development against a
# throwaway database — never set it expecting it to reach production data.
DEV_AUTH=

# --- Free-beta E2: operator credential for the curation skills ---
# The five review skills drive admin-only routes. These two are what
# scripts/operator-curl.sh reads, and they are what lets a skill operate the
# DEPLOYED service instead of a laptop pointed at the production database
# (docs/operator-tooling.md — that pattern is E1's stopgap, now superseded).
#
# OPERATOR_BASE_URL — the service the skills talk to. NO DEFAULT, deliberately:
#   a silent fallback to localhost is how you drain the wrong queue.
#     https://learning-app-sau6bxtxta-uw.a.run.app   production
#     http://localhost:3000                          local dev (DEV_AUTH covers auth)
# OPERATOR_ADMIN_TOKEN — `openssl rand -base64 32`. Only read from this file,
#   never exported into the shell (AGENTS.md: a command that fails while holding
#   a secret may print it). The server rejects anything under 32 bytes.
# OPERATOR_ADMIN_USER_ID — the User.id the token authenticates AS. That row must
#   have role='admin' (assigned by hand: UPDATE "User" SET role='admin' …), and
#   the role is re-checked on every request, so revoking it revokes the token
#   with no redeploy. Both vars must be set or the token path stays inert.
# In cloud, the token and user id are Secret Manager mounts (cloudbuild.yaml);
# OPERATOR_BASE_URL is a local-only setting and is not deployed.
OPERATOR_BASE_URL=
OPERATOR_ADMIN_TOKEN=
OPERATOR_ADMIN_USER_ID=

# (TRACE_RESPONSE was removed with the synchronous path-generation route in the
# playground revamp — no code has read it since. Don't reintroduce the name.)

# --- Model overrides ---
# MODEL_EMBEDDING overrides the embedding model (src/lib/ai/models.ts). Its
# output dimensionality MUST match the vector(768) column in the Resource
# migration — a mismatch fails at embed time, after the model call is billed.
# Leave unset unless you are also migrating the column.
# MODEL_EMBEDDING=

# --- Phase 2.5i: intake session limits (src/lib/services/intake-limits.ts) ---
# All three have code defaults; set them only to tighten or loosen the intake
# rate limit. Worst case at the defaults is ~75 Flash calls per user per hour.
# INTAKE_MAX_TURNS=15
# INTAKE_SESSIONS_PER_HOUR=
# INTAKE_SESSION_WINDOW_MS=

# --- Phase 2.5b-2: YouTube Data API v3 (playlist decomposition) ---
# Plain API key (NOT the Vertex service account). Used server-side to read
# public playlist contents so container playlists can be exploded into atomic
# per-video Resources at discovery time. Recommended GCP key restrictions:
#   Application restrictions: None (serverless egress IPs aren't stable)
#   API restrictions: restrict to "YouTube Data API v3" only
# When unset, playlist decomposition is skipped and the container row is parked
# as decompositionStatus='pending' (auto-retryable once the key is added).
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# --- Phase 3b: Supabase Auth (Google OAuth via @supabase/ssr) ---
# Source: Supabase → Project Settings → API. Same project as DATABASE_URL.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* because the browser client needs them; the anon key is safe to
# expose by design (authorization is enforced in app code, not RLS).
# Google provider setup: Supabase Dashboard → Authentication → Providers →
# Google (client id/secret from the GCP OAuth consent screen), then add
#   http://localhost:3000/auth/callback  and the prod-domain callback
# to Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs.
# When BOTH are unset, auth is off: src/proxy.ts passes through and
# withAuth falls back to the dev bypass (DEV_AUTH above).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Service-role key intentionally NOT listed: nothing server-side needs to
# bypass auth (Prisma talks to Postgres directly). Add it only with a feature
# that needs it, per the JIT-dependency rule.

# --- Phase 3 hardening H2: CSRF origin check ---
# Optional. State-changing API requests must carry an Origin header matching
# the request's own host (derived from x-forwarded-host/Host); requests with
# no Origin header pass. Set this to the canonical public origin (e.g.
# https://app.example.com) only if the public domain differs from what the
# proxy forwards — usually unnecessary on Vercel.
# APP_ORIGIN=

# --- Free-beta D3: how these map onto Cloud Run ---
# The deployed app takes NOTHING from a file. See docs/app-deploy.md for the
# authoritative contract; the split is:
#   build args (inlined into the client bundle by `next build`, so the image is
#     bound to one Supabase project): NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL,
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#   Secret Manager mounts: DATABASE_URL (supabase-database-url, the 6543
#     pooler), YOUTUBE_API_KEY (youtube-api-key)
#   plain --set-env-vars: GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT, GOOGLE_VERTEX_LOCATION
#   deliberately UNSET: GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON — its absence is
#     what selects the ADC path, authenticating as the runtime service account
#   never set: DEV_AUTH (inert anyway — the standalone server hardcodes
#     NODE_ENV=production)
# DIRECT_URL is not a runtime variable at all: it is Prisma-CLI-only, and in
# cloud it exists solely as the supabase-direct-url secret, readable by the
# Cloud Build service account for the `prisma migrate deploy` step.

# --- Phase 3: Stripe ---
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# --- Free-beta B1: error reporting ---
# LOG_SERVICE_NAME names the process in Error Reporting's serviceContext. Unset
# locally and unset on Cloud Run, where K_SERVICE (auto-injected) takes
# precedence; it exists for the worker, which runs as a plain container with no
# K_SERVICE and would otherwise group its errors under the app's service.
# Dockerfile.worker bakes it in, so it does not belong in .env.local either.
# LOG_SERVICE_NAME=course-worker
